  - software: FARGO-3D
  - parameter: grid 19-285 au, 1766 log radial cells, 4096 azimuthal cells; aspect ratio 0.1(R/100 au)^0.225 [Comparison with hydrodynamical simulati…: "using 1766 logarithmically spaced cells in radius and 4096 cells in azimuth"]
  - assumption: viscosity alpha = 0 [Comparison with hydrodynamical simulati…: "are inviscid (i.e. the viscosity, $\alpha$, is null)"]
  - validation: Qualitative comparison of gap width/depth with C18O data [Comparison with hydrodynamical simulati…: "This is commensurate with our C$^{18}$O observations and the results of Section [4](#modeling)."]
- result: Inferred planet mass = 0.2-0.3 M_Jupiter at about 100 au ± not stated M_Jupiter [Conclusions: "formation of a planet of 0.2–0.3 M$_{Jupiter}$ at about 100 au is occurring in the AS 209 protoplanetary disk"]
- limitation (authors): Model does not fully reproduce observations because of 2D temperature structure uncertainties [Modelling results: "our present modelling does not fully reproduce the observations because of uncertainties on the 2D temperature structure"]
- limitation (authors): Insufficient sensitivity to see the co-rotation bump [Comparison with hydrodynamical simulati…: "This feature is not seen in our data because we do not have sufficient sensitivity."]
- not stated (card): Self-calibration solution intervals, number of rounds, and reference models; Line-free channels and continuum subtraction details; CLEAN mask, threshold, cell and image size; Gas-to-dust mass ratio and Sigma_c,gas values for the DALI grid; Statistic used to compare model and data (best values appear chosen by eye); Radial profile bin width and averaging method; Cycle 2 and Cycle 4 data weighting …

### 2018ApJ...866L...6C: High-resolution Millimeter Imaging of the CI Tau Protoplanetary Disk: A Massive Ensemble of Protoplanets from 0.1 to 100 au (2018; protoplanetary_disks, exoplanets; observational_alma_primary)
- question: What is the continuum structure of the CI Tau disc at high resolution, and can its annular gaps be explained by massive planets accompanying the hot Jupiter?
- step 3: Hydrodynamic modelling of gaps with planets
  - method: 2D FARGO3D with dust, synthetic emissivity compared to fitted profile, then synthetic image via CASA ft and CLEAN [4 Modeling: "using the 2D version of the FARGO3D code (cite: fargo3d) with our implementation of drag coupled dust (cite: Rosotti2016)"]
  - software: FARGO3D, CASA
  - parameter: alpha=0.014, disc mass 20 MJ within 200 au, 350x512 cells, 5.6-378 au [4 Modeling: "parameters $\alpha=0.014$ and total disc mass within 200 au of $20 \,M_\mathrm{Jupiter}$"]
  - assumption: fragmentation velocity 10 m/s; dust-to-gas 0.01; a^-3 size distribution of compact silicate grains [4 Modeling: "We assume an initial dust to gas ratio of 0.01."]
  - validation: Compare with accretion rate and spectral index [4 Modeling: "yield a profile of $a_{max}$ that is compatible with measurements of the disc-averaged spectral index in CI Tau"]
- result: Planet masses at 14, 43, 108 au = 0.75, 0.15, 0.4 ± factor ~2 inner, ~30% outer two MJupiter [4 Modeling: "planets of mass 0.75, 0.15 and 0.4 $M_\mathrm{Jupiter}$ are located at orbital radii of 14, 43, and 108 au"]
- limitation (authors): Innermost gap poorly resolved; shape unconstrained [3 Visibility modelling: "Thus we cannot firmly constrain the detailed shape of the innermost gap"]
- not stated (card): Cell size, image size, masks, CLEAN threshold; Cleaning/imaging details of the modelling visibilities (averaging, any self-calibration); Prior ranges for the parameters; Target coordinates and distance used; Details of the dust opacity table and temperature profile; Flux calibrator and flux scale uncertainty

### 2016ApJ...819..134B: Planetary Signatures in the SAO 206462 (HD 135344B) Disk: A Spiral Arm Passing through Vortex? (2016; protoplanetary_disks; theory_or_simulation_compared_to_alma)
- question: Can a planetary-mass companion in the outer disk of SAO 206462 produce the observed spiral arms, asymmetric sub-mm continuum and dust-depleted inner cavity?
- step 1: Run 2D gas+dust hydrodynamic simulations
  - method: Two-fluid FARGO with planet; 100 orbits; planet mass and semi-major axis varied [3 RESULTS: "All the calculations were run for $100~T_p$ where $T_p$ refers to the orbital time at the planet’s location."]
  - software: FARGO
  - parameter: alpha = 1e-4; (H/R)_p = 0.1; Sigma ∝ R^-1; dust-to-gas 1% [2.1 Disk Model: "For the gas component a uniform viscosity parameter $\alpha=10^{-4}$ is implemented."]
  - assumption: Dust size distribution dn ∝ a^-3.5 da between 0.1 um and 5.3 mm; grain density 3 g cm^-3 [2.2 Dust Component: "We assume that the dust density is $3~{\rm g~cm^{-3}}$."]
  - validation: Resolution doubled for 10 MJ planet at 100 au; consistent morphology and radial profiles [Resolution Test: "While the high-resolution run shows more small-scale structures, the overall morphology is in good agreement with the low-resolution run"]
- result: Planet mass preferred = 10-15 ± not stated MJ [abstract: "We suggest that a planetary companion with a mass of 10–15 $M_J$ is orbiting SAO 206462 at 100–120 au."]
- result: Planet orbital radius = 100-120 ± not stated au [abstract: "We suggest that a planetary companion with a mass of 10–15 $M_J$ is orbiting SAO 206462 at 100–120 au."]
- result: Peak dust-to-gas ratio in vortex = ~1:25 ± not stated mass ratio [3 RESULTS: "the dust-to-gas mass ratio inside the vortex becomes as large as $\sim1:25$"]
- result: Vortex total mass at 50 Tp = about 3 ± not stated MJ [3 RESULTS: "it is about $3~M_J$ at $50~T_p$"]
- result: Horseshoe width = about 25 ± not stated au [3 RESULTS: "The width of the horseshoe region ($x_{\rm hs}$) is about 25 au"]
- limitation (authors): 2D only; scattered light compared only qualitatively [4 DISCUSSION: "Thus, we compare the morphology of the arms and density distribution in a qualitative manner, rather than in a quantitative manner."]
- not stated (card): Observation duration and integration time passed to simobserve; Antenna configuration file and exact simobserve/cleaning parameters (weighting, robust); Which simulation snapshot time was used for each synthetic image; Dust opacity composition/optical constants for Mie calculation; Code release of modified FARGO

### 2019ApJ...879L...2M: PDS 70: A Transition Disk Sculpted by a Single Planet (2019; protoplanetary_disks, exoplanets; theory_or_simulation_compared_to_alma)
- question: Can a single accreting super-Jupiter (PDS 70b) carve the observed ~30 au gas and ~60 au dust cavity of the PDS 70 transition disk?
- step 1: Run 2D hydrodynamic simulations
  - method: PEnGUIn with accretion prescription, planet migrating and accreting from 10 Earth masses at 23 au, run to 4.6 Myr; alpha0=0.001, Sigma0=3.86 g/cm2, domain 5-100 au [Results: "best-fit fiducial model, in which the planet is free to both migrate and accrete, with $\alpha_0 = 0.001$ and $\Sigma_0 = \SI{3.86}{\gram\per\cm\squared}$"]
  - software: PEnGUIn
  - parameter: 960 x 1944 cells, 20 cells per scale height at 22 au [Resolution: "960 ($r$) $\times$ 1944 ($\phi$) cells"]
  - assumption: M* = 0.76 Msun, 2D, fixed boundaries [HYDRODYNAMICS SIMULATIONS: "Radial boundaries are fixed to their initial values"]
  - validation: Resolution tests +-50% give planet position within 8% [Resolution: "we find that the position of the planet agrees to within 8%"]
- result: Planet eccentricity saturation = 0.25 ± not stated dimensionless [Disk-Planet Evolution: "which eventually saturates at $e_{\rm p} \approx 0.25$"]
- result: Planet mass for eccentricity onset = 2.7 ± not stated MJ [Table: "960 $\times$ 1944 & 5–100 & 1 & 100 & 4600000 & 23 & 21 & 2.7 & Fiducial"]
- result: Gas depleted below 0.01 g/cm2 out to = 30 ± not stated au [Results: "the planet has depleted the gas below $\Sigma = \SI{0.01}{\gram\per\cm\squared}$ out to 30 au, and produced a pressure peak at 62 au"]
